### [07:25:00 UTC] Step 2 — Hypothesize + experiment (threshold sweep)
Input: Diagnostic evidence from prior cycle (34-78dB IST-vs-baseline gap in 2-19kHz bands on real audio; only 0.95% of ist_changes bins classify "dominant").
Hypothesis A (initialize_ist time-domain gate starves broadband content): tested via scratch script measuring initialize_ist's time-domain survival fraction for a single tone vs a 9-tone broadband synthetic signal at threshold=0.6..0.05. REFUTED: survival fractions were comparable (64.9% broadband vs 58.5% single-tone at threshold=0.6) -- not the bottleneck for this signal class.
Hypothesis B (the frequency-selective peak-inflation cap, _cap_ist_changes_to_baseline_peak, is suppressing non-dominant bands): tested via scratch script comparing ist_changes magnitude per frequency band before vs after capping on a loud-100/300Hz + quiet-800..18000Hz broadband synthetic signal. REFUTED: cap left every band from 800Hz-18000Hz at exactly 100% survival (only the truly dominant 100/300Hz bands were shrunk, to ~8.6%) -- confirms, does not contradict, the existing changelog root-cause text that _ist_chain's own FFT-domain peak-relative threshold (not the cap) is the limiting mechanism for non-dominant bands.
Hypothesis C (lowering threshold_value, unchanged algorithm, increases non-dominant-band IST contribution): tested via scratch sweep (threshold in 0.6, 0.3, 0.15, 0.05, 0.02, 0.01) on the same broadband synthetic signal, measuring IST-vs-baseline dB per band. CONFIRMED at threshold=0.15: every band from 800Hz-18000Hz improved by 8.4-24dB (uniformly ~8.4dB for 2000-18000Hz) vs the current default 0.6, while the two dominant bands (100/300Hz) worsened by <2dB (negligible, and already governed by the separate peak cap). Going lower than 0.15 (0.05, 0.02, 0.01) did NOT continue improving monotonically -- non-dominant bands stayed flat or regressed slightly, so 0.15 is a genuine local optimum in this test, not "lower is always better."
Hypothesis D (algorithm-level fix: octave/bin-index-doubling band-relative FFT threshold instead of one global peak reference): prototyped standalone (not shipped) via scratch script. Showed dramatic gains where a band contained an isolated tone (e.g. 18000Hz: -10.46dB -> +13.13dB at threshold=0.15) but ALSO produced a new, more severe artifact not present in the current mechanism: near-total collapse (-265 to -294dB, i.e. numerically dead) for bands whose content depended on where an arbitrary octave-boundary happened to fall relative to the block's actual spectral content, and interaction with the still-global initialize_ist time-domain gate. Judged INCONCLUSIVE/too risky to ship without audio-quality-checker validation on real, continuous-spectrum program material (my synthetic sparse-tone test cannot rule out this being a regression in disguise on real audio) -- reported as a candidate for a future, more carefully (audio-quality-checker-in-the-loop) validated cycle, not shipped this cycle.
Reasoning: Root-cause the changelog gap precisely (which stage actually limits non-dominant-band content) before proposing or shipping anything, per the scientific-coding loop; prefer a small, verified, reversible change (Hypothesis C) over an unverified architecture change (Hypothesis D) given no access to the real-audio audio-quality-checker pipeline this cycle.
Output: Scratch scripts (not part of the repo) in the session scratchpad: explore_threshold.py, explore_subband.py, explore_init_survival.py, explore_cap_effect.py. Decision: add a durable regression test capturing Hypothesis C's verified property in fat_llama/tests/test_feed.py, and propose threshold_value=0.15 via proposed_upscale_params rather than silently changing upscale()'s hardcoded default (per scientific-coding.md's "you are proposing, not verifying" rule for parameter-only levers).

### [07:40:00 UTC] Step 3 — Implement + verify
Input: Confirmed Hypothesis C (lower threshold_value) and refuted Hypotheses A/B, per Step 2.
Action: Added test_lower_threshold_increases_nondominant_band_ist_contribution to fat_llama/tests/test_feed.py -- verifies (a) the peak-inflation cap leaves non-dominant bands >95% intact (confirms it is not the bottleneck) and (b) threshold_value=0.15 vs 0.6 increases every non-dominant band's own capped IST contribution by >1.5x (+3.5dB) on a 9-tone broadband synthetic signal, plus sanity bounds on the dominant bands and confirmation the cap still functions at the lower threshold. Ran it in isolation (pass, 0.54s), then documented the finding (no behavior change) in iterative_soft_thresholding's own docstring, matching the module's existing per-cycle documentation convention. Ran the full suite (python -m unittest discover -s fat_llama/tests) after each change.
